latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Identify Distinct Shapes:\n   a. Use a flood-fill algorithm to identify all connected non-zero regions in the grid.\n   b. Label each distinct shape with a unique identifier.\n\n2. Analyze Each Shape:\n   a. Calculate the size (number of cells) of each shape.\n   b. Identify the border cells of each shape.\n   c. Determine the center or centers (for complex shapes) of each shape.\n   d. Find the highest color value present in each shape.\n\n3. Determine Color Progression:\n   a. Create a color sequence: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]\n   b. For each shape, determine the target highest color:\n      - If shape size < 4, use the current highest color.\n      - If 4 <= shape size < 9, use the next color in the sequence.\n      - If shape size >= 9, use the color two steps ahead in the sequence.\n\n4. Apply Color Transformation:\n   a. For each shape:\n      - Keep the border cells at their original color or the next color in the sequence for larger shapes.\n      - Fill the cells adjacent to the border with the next color in the sequence.\n      - Fill the center area with the target highest color determined in step 3.\n   b. For shapes with size < 4, simply fill the entire shape with the highest color present.\n\n5. Handle Special Cases:\n   a. For shapes with initially high-value colors (e.g., 3 or 4 in a mostly 1 shape):\n      - Expand this high-value color to fill more of the shape, but not necessarily all of it.\n   b. For complex shapes (like crosses or stars):\n      - Identify sub-structures (e.g., arms of a cross).\n      - Apply color progression to each sub-structure independently.\n\n6. Balance Color Distribution:\n   a. Count the occurrences of each color in the entire grid.\n   b. If any color is overrepresented, replace some instances with adjacent colors in the sequence.\n   c. Ensure that larger structures have more color variety than smaller ones.\n\n7. Maintain Structural Integrity:\n   a. Verify that all connections present in the original shape are maintained.\n   b. Ensure that the overall form of each shape remains unchanged.\n\n8. Refine Borders:\n   a. Smooth color transitions at the borders of regions.\n   b. Ensure that border cells have appropriate colors to maintain structure visibility.\n\n9. Final Checks and Adjustments:\n   a. Verify that no cell has a lower color value than its original.\n   b. Ensure that the grid dimensions match the input.\n   c. Make small adjustments to balance color distribution if necessary.\n\n10. Handle Edge Cases:\n    a. If a color in the input is not in the color sequence (e.g., 0), maintain its original value.\n    b. For very small grids or shapes, simplify the color progression to avoid overcomplexity.\n\nThis step-by-step plan takes into account the patterns observed across all examples and should be flexible enough to handle various grid sizes and shape configurations. It maintains the key aspects of structure preservation, color progression, and balanced distribution while allowing for the nuances observed in different cases."
